NAME
       semanage - change lasting SELinux settings: file contexts, ports, booleans

SYNOPSIS
       semanage fcontext {-a | -m} -t TYPE 'PATTERN'
       semanage fcontext -d 'PATTERN'
       semanage fcontext -a -e REFERENCE PATH
       semanage fcontext -l [-C]
       semanage port {-a | -m} -t TYPE -p tcp|udp PORT
       semanage port -d -p tcp|udp PORT
       semanage port -l [-C]
       semanage boolean -m {--on | --off} BOOLEAN
       semanage boolean -l [-C]
       semanage login -l

DESCRIPTION
       semanage changes the local customisations of the SELinux policy. They
       are saved in the policy store and survive reboots and full relabels.
       Each kind of setting is a subcommand. semanage must be run as root,
       even for listing (it reads the policy store, which only root may
       open).

   fcontext
       Rules that say which context files under a path should get. Adding a
       rule does not relabel anything by itself: run restorecon afterwards.
       The pattern is a regular expression for the whole path. The usual
       form for a directory and everything in it is '/dir(/.*)?'. Quote it
       so the shell does not touch it.

   port
       Which port numbers a service is allowed to use. For example, Apache
       (httpd_t) may bind only to ports of type http_port_t (80, 443, 8008,
       8443...). To run a web server on port 82, add 82 to http_port_t; to
       let sshd listen on 2222, add it to ssh_port_t. A port that already
       has a type in the policy must be changed with -m instead of -a.

   boolean
       The same switches as setsebool -P, with a list that shows each
       boolean's current and default state and a description.

OPTIONS
       -a, --add
              Add a rule.

       -d, --delete
              Delete a rule you added (policy rules cannot be deleted).

       -m, --modify
              Change an existing rule.

       -l, --list
              List the rules, the policy's and yours.

       -C, --locallist
              With -l, list only the rules you added.

       -t, --type TYPE
              The SELinux type, such as httpd_sys_content_t or http_port_t.

       -p, --proto tcp|udp
              The protocol of a port rule.

       -e, --equal REFERENCE
              File context: label PATH the same way as REFERENCE.

       --on, --off
              Boolean: the new value.

EXAMPLES
       Let Apache serve files from /srv/web:

           semanage fcontext -a -t httpd_sys_content_t '/srv/web(/.*)?'
           restorecon -Rv /srv/web

       Show your own file context rules:

           semanage fcontext -l -C

       Let httpd listen on TCP port 82:

           semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 82
           semanage port -l | grep http_port_t

       Remove that port rule again:

           semanage port -d -p tcp 82

       Turn a boolean on for good:

           semanage boolean -m --on httpd_enable_homedirs

NOTES
       semanage comes with the policycoreutils-python-utils package. The
       audit log and sealert usually suggest the exact semanage command for
       a denial; read it before you type it.

SEE ALSO
